1
00:00:08,985 --> 00:00:10,987
[BURTON] Our son's fever
isn't coming down!

2
00:00:11,029 --> 00:00:12,573
And he's having trouble
breathing!

3
00:00:12,615 --> 00:00:15,284
If this keeps up, I don't
think-- Please, I beg you!

4
00:00:15,325 --> 00:00:16,493
Help my boy!

5
00:00:16,535 --> 00:00:18,454
[LORD BELFOR] Quiet down,
Burton. What's the fuss?

6
00:00:18,495 --> 00:00:21,874
It's my son, your lordship!
His fever is getting worse!

7
00:00:21,916 --> 00:00:22,750
[LORD BELFOR]
Then you should take him

8
00:00:22,792 --> 00:00:24,794
to the village
doctor immediately.

9
00:00:24,835 --> 00:00:25,961
[BURTON]
We would,

10
00:00:26,002 --> 00:00:28,380
but, you see, your lordship,
he's not in tonight.

11
00:00:28,422 --> 00:00:30,048
He's away at his
daughter's wedding.

12
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:32,301
All we need is a moment
of your doctor's time.

13
00:00:32,342 --> 00:00:34,136
[LORD BELFOR] It is
simply out of the question.

14
00:00:34,177 --> 00:00:38,515
Expecting my personal physician
to attend to your ailing child.

15
00:00:38,557 --> 00:00:41,059
But if nothing's done,
my boy's likely to die!

16
00:00:41,101 --> 00:00:43,061
[LORD BELFOR]
So what if he does?

17
00:00:43,103 --> 00:00:44,980
I don't see how
it's any of my concern

18
00:00:45,021 --> 00:00:47,023
what happens to
the lower classes.

19
00:00:47,065 --> 00:00:49,777
You think you're entitled
special treatment?

20
00:00:49,819 --> 00:00:51,069
Let me be clear.

21
00:00:51,111 --> 00:00:55,198
I employ you based solely on
your skills as a gardener.

22
00:00:55,240 --> 00:00:58,034
However, bother me with
trivial matters again,

23
00:00:58,076 --> 00:01:01,914
and I will replace you.

24
00:01:01,956 --> 00:01:04,541
Your lordship!
If nothing's done, he'll--

25
00:01:09,964 --> 00:01:13,008
[gasps]

26
00:01:14,844 --> 00:01:17,012
[grunts]

27
00:03:28,602 --> 00:03:29,937
[WILLIAM]
Beg your pardon,

28
00:03:29,979 --> 00:03:32,940
but could I trouble you for
directions to Goetsbeck Manor?

29
00:03:32,982 --> 00:03:36,110
[SUZANNA]
Goetsbeck Manor? Let me guess.

30
00:03:36,151 --> 00:03:39,989
You wouldn't be that university
professor now, would you?

31
00:03:40,030 --> 00:03:43,659
[WILLIAM]
Yes, but how did you know that?

32
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:45,619
[SUZANNA] You're not
in the city anymore, lad.

33
00:03:45,661 --> 00:03:49,456
Out here, word travels faster
than the carriage ride over.

34
00:03:49,498 --> 00:03:52,084
[SUZANNA] Not to mention,
you come from noble stock,

35
00:03:52,126 --> 00:03:54,044
yet you still teach at a school.

36
00:03:54,086 --> 00:03:57,006
It would've been peculiar
not hearing about you!

37
00:03:57,047 --> 00:03:59,675
It would? I suppose so.

38
00:04:03,012 --> 00:04:05,848
[MICHELLE grunts]

39
00:04:11,186 --> 00:04:15,565
You'll have to forgive that one.
She's not fond of aristocrats.

40
00:04:15,607 --> 00:04:19,569
Poor, Michelle. Can't say
I really blame her either.

41
00:04:19,611 --> 00:04:22,031
Not after that business
with the Viscount.

42
00:04:22,072 --> 00:04:24,449
The man basically
stood by and watched

43
00:04:24,491 --> 00:04:26,952
as her son lay
dying in her arms.

44
00:04:26,994 --> 00:04:28,662
[WILLIAM grunts]

45
00:04:32,082 --> 00:04:34,668
Oh! You're back.

46
00:04:34,710 --> 00:04:36,252
Take the scenic route?

47
00:04:36,294 --> 00:04:38,380
I decided to explore
the city streets

48
00:04:38,421 --> 00:04:40,716
and ended up getting
turned around.

49
00:04:40,757 --> 00:04:42,509
Sounds like quite an ordeal.

50
00:04:42,551 --> 00:04:44,553
Since you're back,
care for some tea?

51
00:04:44,594 --> 00:04:46,722
I'll go and prepare some.

52
00:04:50,809 --> 00:04:52,019
[WILLIAM]
Mm.

53
00:04:52,061 --> 00:04:55,189
It feels good to be officially
settled in one's home.

54
00:04:55,230 --> 00:04:58,233
I'm not sure I would
refer to this place as "home."

55
00:04:58,274 --> 00:05:01,570
Other than the quick inspection
we made before purchasing it,

56
00:05:01,611 --> 00:05:04,198
this is your first time
staying at the house.

57
00:05:04,239 --> 00:05:06,282
True, this residence
is temporary

58
00:05:06,324 --> 00:05:09,411
and was chosen based on how
close it is to the university.

59
00:05:09,452 --> 00:05:11,997
But location is irrelevant.

60
00:05:12,039 --> 00:05:16,501
Anywhere that I'm able to drink
your tea... I'll call my home.

61
00:05:16,543 --> 00:05:19,588
[LOUIS grunts]

62
00:05:19,629 --> 00:05:25,343
Oh. That reminds me.
This came for you earlier.

63
00:05:25,385 --> 00:05:27,054
From the Viscount of Belfor?

64
00:05:27,096 --> 00:05:28,263
[LOUIS]
Yes.

65
00:05:28,304 --> 00:05:31,808
Apparently, he's the lord
over this entire region.

66
00:05:31,850 --> 00:05:34,269
[WILLIAM] How neighborly.
We've only just arrived

67
00:05:34,310 --> 00:05:38,023
and he's already
extending a welcoming hand.

68
00:05:38,065 --> 00:05:41,860
Remind me, Louis. Is Albert
still due to arrive tonight?

69
00:05:41,902 --> 00:05:45,739
He is. He said he would pay a
visit to see how everything was

70
00:05:45,781 --> 00:05:48,200
after he finished
his current job.

71
00:05:48,242 --> 00:05:50,035
I see.

72
00:05:50,077 --> 00:05:53,538
Hope the Viscount doesn't mind
if I bring two guests.

73
00:06:05,092 --> 00:06:08,804
[LORD BELFOR chewing] Mm!
I must say, this is a delight!

74
00:06:08,845 --> 00:06:11,181
Cheers! To you
and your two brothers!

75
00:06:11,223 --> 00:06:13,642
Having you at my table
is a true honor!

76
00:06:13,683 --> 00:06:15,144
As fellow noblemen,

77
00:06:15,185 --> 00:06:18,897
I hope we can enjoy each other's
company for many years to come.

78
00:06:18,939 --> 00:06:20,983
As do we, your lordship.

79
00:06:21,024 --> 00:06:23,652
[LORD BELFOR] What an evening!
Positively smashing!

80
00:06:23,693 --> 00:06:27,239
More? We're celebrating! Mm.

81
00:06:27,281 --> 00:06:29,574
Your lordship,
I think that's enough.

82
00:06:29,616 --> 00:06:32,077
Ugh. Very well.

83
00:06:32,119 --> 00:06:33,495
[BUTLER 04A]
It's nearly time for your dosage

84
00:06:33,536 --> 00:06:35,413
of medicine as well.

85
00:06:35,455 --> 00:06:37,040
Have you been feeling ill?

86
00:06:37,082 --> 00:06:40,169
[LORD BELFOR] It's nothing.
Just a spot of heart trouble.

87
00:06:40,210 --> 00:06:41,795
Sounds serious.

88
00:06:41,837 --> 00:06:44,589
Please. There's no
cause for any alarm

89
00:06:44,631 --> 00:06:46,675
as long as I have this bottle.

90
00:06:46,716 --> 00:06:48,551
What's inside?

91
00:06:49,803 --> 00:06:52,014
This elixir goes
by the name quinine.

92
00:06:52,055 --> 00:06:55,433
It was originally a
treatment used for malaria.

93
00:06:55,475 --> 00:06:58,020
But my personal physician
says it's also effective for

94
00:06:58,061 --> 00:07:00,105
combating certain
heart ailments.

95
00:07:00,147 --> 00:07:01,190
Huh.

96
00:07:01,231 --> 00:07:02,732
[LORD BELFOR]
It might interest you to know

97
00:07:02,774 --> 00:07:04,860
this quinine is
actually homemade.

98
00:07:04,901 --> 00:07:07,154
The main ingredient derives
from the quina plant,

99
00:07:07,196 --> 00:07:10,532
which I happen to grow here,
in my conservatory.

100
00:07:10,573 --> 00:07:12,659
I take it horticulture's
one of your interests?

101
00:07:12,701 --> 00:07:15,411
Ah. Well, yes. Though,

102
00:07:15,453 --> 00:07:18,665
I'm honestly more interested
in the collecting aspect.

103
00:07:18,707 --> 00:07:20,042
How fortunate.

104
00:07:20,083 --> 00:07:23,419
Seems this hobby of yours
has yielded unexpected benefits.

105
00:07:23,461 --> 00:07:27,799
Huh. You know, my
collection's quite vast.

106
00:07:27,841 --> 00:07:29,134
After we finish dinner,

107
00:07:29,176 --> 00:07:32,012
would you all care to
view the conservatory?

108
00:07:32,054 --> 00:07:35,057
Yes. By all means.

109
00:07:39,769 --> 00:07:42,064
Are you impressed?

110
00:07:42,105 --> 00:07:44,274
This truly is a sight to behold.

111
00:07:44,316 --> 00:07:47,903
[LORD BELFOR chuckles]
Note the variety of specimens.

112
00:07:47,944 --> 00:07:51,240
Each one requires a specific
habitat and growing method.

113
00:07:51,281 --> 00:07:53,200
Otherwise, it wouldn't thrive.

114
00:07:53,242 --> 00:07:55,702
They do, however,
have something in common.

115
00:07:55,744 --> 00:07:57,329
All of these plants are rare,

116
00:07:57,371 --> 00:08:00,874
only able to spring forth
from an exceptional seed.

117
00:08:00,916 --> 00:08:03,919
Quite similar to people.

118
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:07,130
Wouldn't you agree, gentlemen?

119
00:08:07,172 --> 00:08:09,465
So many. I imagine
caring for them all

120
00:08:09,507 --> 00:08:11,885
must be a rather
time-consuming endeavor.

121
00:08:11,927 --> 00:08:14,263
Certainly, but I have
an exceptional gardener

122
00:08:14,304 --> 00:08:15,931
overseeing their upkeep.

123
00:08:15,972 --> 00:08:19,184
Speaking of,
there goes the man now.

124
00:08:19,226 --> 00:08:23,146
Everything you see here
is tended to by him.

125
00:08:23,188 --> 00:08:26,191
Name's Burton and while he's
not a very considerate chap,

126
00:08:26,233 --> 00:08:29,403
there's no denying he has a gift
when it comes to plants.

127
00:08:29,444 --> 00:08:31,446
From the sweat of
the lower class,

128
00:08:31,487 --> 00:08:34,657
the noble stock is
able to fully bloom.

129
00:08:34,699 --> 00:08:35,825
You might even say

130
00:08:35,867 --> 00:08:39,121
this conservatory is
a microcosm of our society!

131
00:08:39,162 --> 00:08:42,999
[LORD BELFOR laughing]

132
00:08:43,041 --> 00:08:45,252
Listen, I just had a thought.

133
00:08:45,294 --> 00:08:48,171
Why don't we have a
tea party in here sometime?

134
00:08:48,213 --> 00:08:51,174
It'll be as though we died
and went to paradise.

135
00:08:51,216 --> 00:08:54,136
[LORD BELFOR laughs]

136
00:08:59,015 --> 00:09:01,893
Uh...

137
00:09:01,935 --> 00:09:04,604
Do you mind explaining?

138
00:09:04,646 --> 00:09:08,691
No one else lives here
besides you and me.

139
00:09:08,733 --> 00:09:11,861
So why did you set three places?

140
00:09:13,029 --> 00:09:16,366
[MICHELLE]
It felt right. I had a dream.

141
00:09:16,408 --> 00:09:20,578
Our boy was crying
because he was hungry.

142
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:24,624
[BURTON'S SON laughing]

143
00:09:24,666 --> 00:09:27,043
Tell me, what's the point
of carrying on like this?

144
00:09:27,085 --> 00:09:29,754
It won't change the past!

145
00:09:29,796 --> 00:09:31,089
[MICHELLE]
You're right.

146
00:09:31,131 --> 00:09:35,760
It won't change what
happened that night.

147
00:09:35,802 --> 00:09:39,139
Or bring him back.

148
00:09:54,154 --> 00:09:56,448
In light of this
being our first class,

149
00:09:56,490 --> 00:09:59,575
how about we end things
here for the day?

150
00:10:01,370 --> 00:10:04,164
An excellent lecture
and easy to follow.

151
00:10:04,206 --> 00:10:05,499
Indeed.

152
00:10:05,540 --> 00:10:08,710
Hard to believe it was delivered
by a professor so young.

153
00:10:08,751 --> 00:10:10,586
[LUCIAN]
Frankly, I'm astounded.

154
00:10:10,628 --> 00:10:14,257
[TATE] Without a doubt,
he's some kind of genius.

155
00:10:24,226 --> 00:10:25,935
[WILLIAM]
Would you mind if I joined you?

156
00:10:25,977 --> 00:10:28,563
[BURTON grunts]

157
00:10:33,776 --> 00:10:36,154
Guess that depends--who're you?

158
00:10:36,196 --> 00:10:39,491
William James Moriarty,
at your service.

159
00:10:39,533 --> 00:10:40,950
I recently moved to your town

160
00:10:40,992 --> 00:10:44,246
to teach mathematics
at the university.

161
00:10:44,287 --> 00:10:46,706
Yeah, I heard about you.

162
00:10:46,748 --> 00:10:50,502
Why would a bigwig professor
want to associate with me?

163
00:10:50,544 --> 00:10:52,003
[WILLIAM]
I've seen you...

164
00:10:52,045 --> 00:10:55,715
...working at the Viscount
of Belfor's conservatory.

165
00:10:55,757 --> 00:10:58,801
You're responsible for tending
to the gardens, is that correct?

166
00:10:58,843 --> 00:11:01,763
Yeah. That's my job, all right.

167
00:11:01,804 --> 00:11:04,724
[WILLIAM] Being able to
raise so many rare varieties,

168
00:11:04,766 --> 00:11:07,102
it must be exceptionally
difficult.

169
00:11:07,143 --> 00:11:09,271
But the results are
truly stunning.

170
00:11:09,312 --> 00:11:11,398
I'm glad you like 'em.

171
00:11:11,440 --> 00:11:13,316
You have a marvelous
talent, sir.

172
00:11:13,358 --> 00:11:16,819
I have no doubt it would be in
demand in the Gardens of London.

173
00:11:16,861 --> 00:11:18,988
[BURTON laughs]
What a load of bollocks.

174
00:11:19,030 --> 00:11:20,990
My father, and
his father before him

175
00:11:21,032 --> 00:11:23,243
served as gardeners
for the Viscount's family.

176
00:11:23,285 --> 00:11:26,121
So, I'm destined to do the same.

177
00:11:26,162 --> 00:11:30,708
We were born here and I have
no doubt that we'll die here.

178
00:11:30,750 --> 00:11:33,587
There's simply no
changing that fact.

179
00:11:33,628 --> 00:11:37,799
One might call it
the story of your life.

180
00:11:37,840 --> 00:11:39,342
Speaking of stories,

181
00:11:39,384 --> 00:11:40,260
I heard about your son.

182
00:11:40,302 --> 00:11:41,844
[BURTON gasps]

183
00:11:41,886 --> 00:11:43,721
[WILLIAM]
Seems your wife hasn't recovered

184
00:11:43,763 --> 00:11:46,933
from her psychological trauma.

185
00:11:46,975 --> 00:11:48,851
Why are you here
instead of by her side--

186
00:11:48,893 --> 00:11:50,353
That's none of your business!

187
00:11:50,395 --> 00:11:52,314
[CUSTOMERS murmur]

188
00:11:56,234 --> 00:12:00,238
The reason I'm not there is
'cause it wouldn't do any good.

189
00:12:00,280 --> 00:12:03,908
She despises me, rightfully so.

190
00:12:03,950 --> 00:12:06,702
I stood by, powerless,

191
00:12:06,744 --> 00:12:09,539
refusing to stand up
to the Viscount,

192
00:12:09,581 --> 00:12:12,959
while my own flesh and blood
was condemned to die.

193
00:12:21,635 --> 00:12:23,094
So, you mistakenly believe

194
00:12:23,136 --> 00:12:26,848
that you were powerless
to save your son.

195
00:12:26,889 --> 00:12:28,600
You chose to do nothing.

196
00:12:28,642 --> 00:12:30,435
[BURTON gasps]

197
00:12:32,854 --> 00:12:35,482
Shut up.

198
00:12:35,524 --> 00:12:39,528
All right then,
what should I have done?

199
00:12:44,991 --> 00:12:48,453
[BURTON]
What can I do to save Michelle?

200
00:12:58,254 --> 00:13:00,632
[BURTON]
Take vengeance.

201
00:13:03,801 --> 00:13:05,345
Seems you're in luck.

202
00:13:05,387 --> 00:13:08,390
You see, Mister Burton,
in addition to my teaching,

203
00:13:08,431 --> 00:13:10,517
I run a consultancy
business on the side

204
00:13:10,559 --> 00:13:12,435
concerning these matters.

205
00:13:12,477 --> 00:13:17,065
Unless I'm mistaken,
I may be of some service to you.

206
00:13:36,084 --> 00:13:37,627
[WILLIAM]
Brother.

207
00:13:37,669 --> 00:13:39,462
[ALBERT]
Finished with your research?

208
00:13:39,504 --> 00:13:42,006
[WILLIAM]
Yes. I found what I needed:

209
00:13:42,048 --> 00:13:44,509
A way of dispelling
the darkness.

210
00:13:44,551 --> 00:13:48,846
Darkness? I take it you're
referring to the Viscount?

211
00:13:48,888 --> 00:13:51,099
No, not him.

212
00:13:51,140 --> 00:13:53,727
I'm talking about
a perpetual gloom.

213
00:13:53,768 --> 00:13:55,853
The kind that
resides in one's soul,

214
00:13:55,895 --> 00:13:59,065
sapping their strength
and will to live.

215
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:03,152
[LORD BELFOR] Welcome, sirs! I
was afraid you wouldn't make it.

216
00:14:03,194 --> 00:14:05,863
And miss having tea
in a literal paradise?

217
00:14:05,905 --> 00:14:07,616
We wouldn't dream
of it, my lord.

218
00:14:07,657 --> 00:14:09,367
Allow me to assure
you gentlemen,

219
00:14:09,409 --> 00:14:14,163
it'll be everything you expected
and much, much more. Hm?

220
00:14:14,205 --> 00:14:16,499
After accepting such
a generous invitation,

221
00:14:16,541 --> 00:14:19,001
it'd be improper to
show up empty handed.

222
00:14:19,043 --> 00:14:21,003
[LOUIS] We brought you
something quite rare.

223
00:14:21,045 --> 00:14:23,131
A gift we thought
would be to your liking.

224
00:14:23,172 --> 00:14:25,174
[LORD BELFOR]
Oh.

225
00:14:33,391 --> 00:14:37,437
[LORD BELFOR] Ah, I'm intrigued.
Appearance wise at least,

226
00:14:37,479 --> 00:14:40,523
it seems to be nothing more
than common marmalade.

227
00:14:40,565 --> 00:14:43,777
Well, you should find the taste
an all-together new experience.

228
00:14:43,818 --> 00:14:46,154
All right.

229
00:14:51,033 --> 00:14:52,494
Such flavour.

230
00:14:52,535 --> 00:14:56,498
Some variety of citrus,
but neither orange nor lemon.

231
00:14:56,539 --> 00:14:57,791
Tell me.

232
00:14:57,832 --> 00:14:58,833
Despite the fruit's rarity,

233
00:14:58,874 --> 00:15:01,419
you wouldn't have
difficulty finding them.

234
00:15:01,461 --> 00:15:04,088
They come from a plant
you're familiar with.

235
00:15:04,130 --> 00:15:05,715
You don't say.

236
00:15:05,757 --> 00:15:07,676
It's grapefruit, Lord Belfor.

237
00:15:07,717 --> 00:15:11,012
You're joking.

238
00:15:11,053 --> 00:15:13,055
This is quite a surprise.

239
00:15:13,097 --> 00:15:15,266
I thought they were merely
pleasurable to look at,

240
00:15:15,308 --> 00:15:18,436
but to think,
they're also delicious!

241
00:15:21,939 --> 00:15:23,899
The flavour's agreeable then?

242
00:15:23,941 --> 00:15:27,696
Very much so. Mm!

243
00:15:27,737 --> 00:15:28,946
There's a slight
bitterness to it

244
00:15:28,988 --> 00:15:31,366
which has the danger of
becoming of a new addiction.

245
00:15:31,407 --> 00:15:33,702
In that case, might I
recommend the juice, as well?

246
00:15:33,743 --> 00:15:36,245
It has a brisk taste
you won't find elsewhere,

247
00:15:36,287 --> 00:15:38,832
particularly when
it's freshly squeezed.

248
00:15:38,873 --> 00:15:40,625
Huh.

249
00:15:44,920 --> 00:15:50,301
[LORD BELFOR laughs] A point
well expressed, if I do say so!

250
00:16:05,734 --> 00:16:08,570
[LORD BELFOR laughs]

251
00:16:08,611 --> 00:16:11,531
[LORD BELFOR laughs]

252
00:16:29,006 --> 00:16:30,383
I had no idea this afternoon

253
00:16:30,425 --> 00:16:33,344
would prove to be
so educational!

254
00:16:35,137 --> 00:16:36,598
[LORD BELFOR]
Hm?

255
00:16:39,225 --> 00:16:40,643
[BURTON]
Michelle! What are you doing?!

256
00:16:40,685 --> 00:16:43,020
[LORD BELFOR gasps, whimpers]

257
00:16:43,062 --> 00:16:45,064
[whimpers]

258
00:16:45,106 --> 00:16:47,275
[MICHELLE groans]

259
00:16:47,316 --> 00:16:50,779
Could someone explain to me
what the hell is going on here?!

260
00:16:50,820 --> 00:16:53,698
After what you did,
you don't deserve to live!

261
00:16:53,740 --> 00:16:56,325
Remember? We begged you
to help our son!

262
00:16:56,367 --> 00:16:58,912
And you just let him die!

263
00:17:02,540 --> 00:17:04,500
Viscount, I swear,

264
00:17:04,542 --> 00:17:08,045
I'll cut out your heart
if you even have one!

265
00:17:08,087 --> 00:17:11,716
[LORD BELFOR gasps]
My medicine, quick!

266
00:17:12,926 --> 00:17:17,096
[WILLIAM] Worry not,
Lord Belfor. I'll retrieve it.

267
00:17:17,138 --> 00:17:18,681
[LORD BELFOR groans]

268
00:17:23,937 --> 00:17:27,064
[BURTON]
Here, your lordship. Drink.

269
00:17:33,362 --> 00:17:35,865
Please accept my
deepest apologies.

270
00:17:35,907 --> 00:17:39,285
I had no idea my wife
would do something so drastic.

271
00:17:40,495 --> 00:17:42,413
[LORD BELFOR groans]
Silence!

272
00:17:42,455 --> 00:17:45,166
Spare me your excuses!
You're fired, Burton!

273
00:17:45,207 --> 00:17:47,293
I want you out of
my sight this instant!

274
00:17:47,335 --> 00:17:48,544
But before you go,

275
00:17:48,586 --> 00:17:51,130
you'll hold that woman
until the authorities arrive!

276
00:17:51,172 --> 00:17:53,883
On second thought,
no need to involve the police

277
00:17:53,925 --> 00:17:58,220
when I can see to a far better
punishment for her myself!

278
00:18:08,940 --> 00:18:10,232
[LORD BELFOR]
The wench!

279
00:18:10,274 --> 00:18:14,153
She had the gall to point
a knife at me, of all things!

280
00:18:14,195 --> 00:18:16,698
But what else can you
expect from the lower class?

281
00:18:16,739 --> 00:18:18,282
[ALBERT]
Please, Lord Belfor.

282
00:18:18,324 --> 00:18:20,952
It isn't good for your heart
to get so worked up.

283
00:18:20,994 --> 00:18:23,245
[WILLIAM] Fortunately,
she didn't cause you any harm.

284
00:18:23,287 --> 00:18:25,957
Perhaps it'd be best to settle
this with them peacefully.

285
00:18:25,999 --> 00:18:27,583
You actually believe
this is a matter

286
00:18:27,625 --> 00:18:29,168
that can be solved peacefully?!

287
00:18:29,210 --> 00:18:32,129
Oh! I swear,
that woman will pay--

288
00:18:32,171 --> 00:18:33,631
Wh-What is this?

289
00:18:33,673 --> 00:18:36,509
Why do I suddenly feel faint?

290
00:18:38,427 --> 00:18:41,055
There's more to plants
than meets the eye.

291
00:18:41,096 --> 00:18:44,350
For example, grapefruits
contain furanocoumarin,

292
00:18:44,392 --> 00:18:47,228
a compound that has a profound
influence on quinine,

293
00:18:47,269 --> 00:18:49,564
resulting in a sharp drop
to blood pressure.

294
00:18:49,605 --> 00:18:53,943
My doctor...
Someone fetch my doctor.

295
00:18:53,985 --> 00:18:55,778
- -[glass shatters]
- -[LORD BELFOR groans]

296
00:18:55,820 --> 00:18:58,072
[WILLIAM] It wasn't long ago
that very same request

297
00:18:58,113 --> 00:19:00,533
was made by
a distraught young couple.

298
00:19:02,284 --> 00:19:03,619
[WILLIAM]
And without hesitation,

299
00:19:03,661 --> 00:19:06,205
you gave them your answer.

300
00:19:06,247 --> 00:19:07,957
[gasping]

301
00:19:07,999 --> 00:19:11,252
Do you remember
what you told them?

302
00:19:11,293 --> 00:19:14,255
[groans]

303
00:19:16,925 --> 00:19:19,176
[LORD BELFOR gasps]

304
00:19:19,218 --> 00:19:20,970
[LORD BELFOR]
You don't mean...

305
00:19:21,012 --> 00:19:24,432
You set me up from
the very beginning?

306
00:19:24,473 --> 00:19:27,393
I wondered when it would
finally dawn on you.

307
00:19:27,435 --> 00:19:29,144
[MICHELLE cries]

308
00:19:29,186 --> 00:19:31,064
Michelle!

309
00:19:31,105 --> 00:19:34,316
You executed your role
perfectly, my darling.

310
00:19:34,358 --> 00:19:38,571
[sobbing]

311
00:19:38,613 --> 00:19:40,364
At Mister Burton's request,

312
00:19:40,406 --> 00:19:43,242
I devised a scenario that would
appear to be a natural death.

313
00:19:43,284 --> 00:19:46,245
I trusted you. How could
you do such a thing?

314
00:19:46,287 --> 00:19:49,040
[WILLIAM]
This is your punishment.

315
00:19:50,374 --> 00:19:52,835
For all the pain
you've inflicted,

316
00:19:52,877 --> 00:19:57,590
I, William James Moriarty,
sentence you to die.

317
00:20:18,069 --> 00:20:20,529
Man of your talents
should find work easily.

318
00:20:20,571 --> 00:20:22,615
Might I recommend
Queen Mary's Garden?

319
00:20:22,657 --> 00:20:24,867
Simply show the
letter I gave you.

320
00:20:24,909 --> 00:20:26,702
I don't know how to
begin repaying you

321
00:20:26,744 --> 00:20:29,747
for giving us a second chance
at life and avenging our son.

322
00:20:29,789 --> 00:20:31,124
There's no need

323
00:20:31,165 --> 00:20:33,167
after everything
you've been through.

324
00:20:33,208 --> 00:20:35,920
The truth is,
the seed was already planted,

325
00:20:35,962 --> 00:20:38,297
and while we helped
to nourish it,

326
00:20:38,339 --> 00:20:41,342
in the end, its growth
and the fruits it yielded

327
00:20:41,383 --> 00:20:42,844
were due to you and you alone.

328
00:20:42,885 --> 00:20:44,386
[BOTH gasp]

329
00:20:47,932 --> 00:20:49,433
[BURTON grunts]

330
00:20:52,937 --> 00:20:54,689
[BURTON chuckles]

331
00:21:16,377 --> 00:21:18,838
[LOUIS] Do you suppose
we've dispelled the darkness?

332
00:21:18,880 --> 00:21:20,882
And they'll be able to carry on?

333
00:21:20,923 --> 00:21:22,466
I don't see why not.

334
00:21:22,508 --> 00:21:24,426
They share the
burden of a crime now--

335
00:21:24,468 --> 00:21:27,555
a bond that's not
so easily broken.

336
00:21:38,691 --> 00:21:41,276
[WILLIAM]
One that tethers them, eternal.

337
00:23:34,974 --> 00:23:38,060
[WILLIAM] Next time
on Moriarty the Patriot  .

338
00:23:38,102 --> 00:23:40,021
"The Dancers on the Bridge."
